# Caddy, with the one module Stem's front door needs that the stock binary does
# not have.
#
# Rate limiting is not in standard Caddy and there is no plan for it to be — it
# lives in github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit, written by Caddy's own author but
# kept out of the default build. That leaves three ways to have it, and this file
# is the choice among them:
#
#   1. Build Caddy with the module (here). One extra stage, no third-party image
#      to trust, and `docker compose build` produces both halves of the deployment
#      from source. Costs a Go toolchain download on first build, cached after.
#   2. Use somebody's prebuilt "caddy with plugins" image. Faster, and it means an
#      unaudited binary terminates TLS for every request to your Stem.
#   3. Do without, and rely on POST /pair's own attempt lockout (pairing.ts) plus
#      the bearer token on everything else. Defensible — the lockout is the check
#      that actually stops a guessed code — but it leaves nothing at all in front
#      of an unauthenticated route, and /rpc unlimited in front of a model.
#
# (1), because a reverse proxy is the one process here that talks to strangers.
#
# If the build is ever the thing standing between you and a running Stem, (3) is
# a two-line edit: drop the `rate_limit` lines from the Caddyfile and change the
# `build:` in docker-compose.yml to `image: caddy:2`. Nothing else depends on it.
FROM caddy:2-builder AS builder
RUN xcaddy build --with github.com/mholt/caddy-ratelimit

FROM caddy:2
COPY --from=builder /usr/bin/caddy /usr/bin/caddy
